<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Share future career plans and aspirations. Interactive: Lead a discussion on various areas including academic topics or past experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can share future career aspirations, explaining the role English will play (e.g., "I want to work in diplomacy, so learning English is essential.")<br>Can lead discussions about the role of English in global workplaces, prompting classmates to share perspectives (e.g., "Why do you think English is important in international business?")<br>Can clarify classmates' points in discussions on global English, ensuring understanding (e.g., "So, you believe English helps people connect across cultures?")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various international workplaces (e.g., a UN conference, a tech company with diverse employees, a global business meeting). Students are prompted to discuss in pairs: "What communication skills do you think are most important in these global workplaces?" and "How has English become so vital in these contexts?" (3-4 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models responses, incorporating target vocabulary and grammar. For example: "I believe *cross-cultural communication* is *necessary*. English *has become* a *lingua franca*, so *fluency* is *important*. Employees *must* be *proficient*." The teacher also models the pronunciation of words like "/ˈrɛlɪvənt/" (relevant) and "/ˌkəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/" (communication), emphasizing the /e/ sound and reduced vowels. (2 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts introduced. (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone has 'fluency' in English, what does that mean?" (Expected answer: They speak it smoothly, well.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'lingua franca' a language used only in one country?" (Expected answer: No, it's used globally for communication.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to an audio dialogue (or read its transcript) between two professionals discussing the role of English in their global careers and the communication skills they employ. The dialogue includes examples of the present perfect, modals for necessity, and indirect questions. (e.g., "English *has spread* globally." "We *must* adapt our communication." "Can you *explain* what *cross-cultural communication* means to you?")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize how English is used to discuss professional topics and express opinions in formal contexts, preparing them to lead discussions and clarify points.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> (5-7 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main topic of the conversation?" (e.g., English and global communication skills).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> (Students decide if statements about the dialogue are true or false).</li>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One speaker believes English is no longer *relevant*." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They both agree that *active listening* is a *vital* communication skill." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The dialogue mentions that companies *should* provide *resources* for language learning." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice speaking using present perfect, modals for necessity ("must," "could," "should," "can"), and indirect questions. They actively use target vocabulary: *English, workplaces, communication, skills, fluency, resources, business, diplomacy, lingua franca, cross-cultural communication, global, interactions, relevant, necessary, important, accessible, vital, proficient, spread, learn, practice, connect, recognize, become, access, explain, communicate, express, enhance, explore, employ*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> (15-18 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences orally using the correct form of verbs (present perfect) and appropriate modals and target vocabulary. Teacher projects sentences like: "English ________ (become) the *dominant* language in *international business*." (has become) "To succeed, employees ________ (must/should) *enhance* their *cross-cultural communication* skills." "Access to *academic resources* ________ (can) really help students *learn* faster." (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Indirect Question Transformation (Semi-controlled Drill):</b> In pairs, students receive a list of direct questions related to global workplaces and communication skills. They take turns transforming these into indirect questions and asking their partner. Teacher emphasizes correct pronunciation, especially multisyllabic words like "communication" and "accessible." For example: <br><i>Direct:</i> "Why is English *important* in *diplomacy*?" <br><i>Indirect:</i> "Can you *explain* why English *has become* such an *important* language for *diplomacy*?" <br><i>Direct:</i> "What skills are *necessary* for *global interactions*?" <br><i>Indirect:</i> "I *wonder* what skills *could* be *necessary* for *global interactions*." (6 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Structured Dialogue (Pair Interaction):</b> Students engage in short, structured dialogues using sentence starters. Partner A asks a question about career aspirations or the role of English, and Partner B responds, then clarifies. The teacher provides sentence frames: "I want to work in [career field], so learning English [must/could/should]..." "Why do you think English [has spread/become] so much?" "So, you believe [restating partner's point]?" "Can you *tell me* what specific *communication skills* *could* help me *advance*?" (7 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Global Career Panel Discussion"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in groups of 4-5 to simulate a panel discussion on "The Role of English and Essential Communication Skills in Future Global Careers." Each student takes on the role of an aspiring professional (e.g., diplomat, international business manager, tech specialist) and shares their future career plans and aspirations, explaining how English will play a *vital* role. One student in each group acts as a moderator, leading the discussion by asking indirect questions and prompting classmates to share perspectives, ensuring *cross-cultural communication* is recognized. Other students in the group are encouraged to ask clarifying questions (e.g., "So, you believe English helps people *connect* across *cultures*?"). (10-12 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, leading and participating in discussions, clarifying points, and communicating future aspirations using formal language in a simulated workplace context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on students' active participation, use of target language (grammar, vocabulary, pronunciation), and ability to lead/participate in discussions and clarify points.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Global Career Panel Discussion" from Stage 4. The teacher circulates and observes each group, making notes on the checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b> (5 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of /e/ sound and full/reduced vowels in multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (*workplaces, communication, skills, fluency, diplomacy, lingua franca, cross-cultural communication, global, relevant, necessary, vital, proficient*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of present perfect, modals for necessity, and indirect questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in sharing career aspirations and explaining English's role.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to lead discussions, prompt classmates, and clarify points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of formal language in workplace contexts.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about *communication skills* in *global workplaces* is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I feel [confident/somewhat confident/not confident yet] discussing my career aspirations and the role of English because..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> (3 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's discussion about *international English* and *workplaces* did you find most engaging? Why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which *communication skill* do you *believe* you *could* improve to *enhance* your future *global interactions*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> (2 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The panel discussion worked well to get students speaking and applying the target structures. Most students were able to share aspirations and use present perfect and modals correctly. Some students struggled slightly with forming indirect questions spontaneously while leading a discussion, and clarifying points succinctly. The pronunciation practice was generally effective, but some students still need more reinforcement for reduced vowels in longer words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For next lesson, I will provide more scaffolding for constructing indirect questions in real-time and provide clearer examples of how to effectively clarify points without interrupting flow. I will also incorporate a quick review of the long /e/ sound and multisyllabic word pronunciation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use the ideas and vocabulary discussed today about career aspirations and communication skills in global workplaces to write a reflective essay or a professional profile. They will transform their spoken ideas into well-structured written compositions, solidifying their understanding and application of the theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>